research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-6943-5.1 The same family shows that $(K,d_K)$ is not totally bounded, which is the property the general characterisation identifies as missing. Suppose $\{y_0,\dots,y_p\} \subseteq K$ were a finite $1/2$-net. Assigning to each $k \in \mathbb{N}$ the least $i \le p$ with $d(e_k,y_i) < 1/2$ gives a map $\mathbb{N} \to \sigma(p)$, which cannot be injective by pigeonhole; so there are $j \ne k$ and one $i$ with $d(e_j,y_i) < 1/2$ and $d(e_k,y_i) < 1/2$, whence $d(e_j,e_k) \le d(e_j,y_i)+d(y_i,e_k) < 1$, contradicting step 1.2. [step 1.2, L6, L8] ∎
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-6944-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-6945-## Remarks
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-6946-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log:6947:- **What is refuted and what is not.** The claim refuted is the transfer of [[thm-heine-borel-rn]] to arbitrary normed spaces. No general classification of normed spaces is asserted here; in particular the classical converse, that a normed space whose closed unit ball is compact must be finite-dimensional, is **not** proved anywhere here.
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-6948-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-6949-- **Why the linear structure does not help.** The bisection proof of [[thm-heine-borel-rn]] halves one coordinate at a time and terminates because there are finitely many coordinates. On $V$ there are infinitely many, and the standard unit vectors stay a fixed distance apart no matter how far out one looks; that is exactly the failure of total boundedness in step 5.1.
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-6950-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-6951-- **The relation to the published metric-space version.** [[fs-closed-and-bounded-implies-compact-in-every-metric-space]] refutes the claim for metric spaces, and its witness is $\mathbb{N}$ carrying the metric that assigns distance $1$ to distinct points — a set with no linear structure at all. The present item refutes the narrower claim about **normed** spaces, on a space that is a linear subspace of a function space and carries a genuine norm, so no reader can retreat to "the counterexample was not linear".
--
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-6995- 
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-6996--- **Nothing is claimed here about general infinite-dimensional normed spaces.** This library has not built that theory. What is exhibited is one real vector space carrying two inequivalent norms, which is all that is needed to refute the claim.
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-6997-+- **No classification of infinite-dimensional normed spaces is claimed here.** What is exhibited is one real vector space carrying two inequivalent norms, which is all that is needed to refute the claim.
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-6998- 
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log:6999: - **Where the proof of [[thm-all-norms-on-rn-are-equivalent]] breaks on $V$.** That proof takes the unit sphere of $\lVert\cdot\rVert_2$, which is closed and bounded, and concludes compactness from [[thm-heine-borel-rn]] — a theorem about $\mathbb{R}^{n}$ for a natural $n$, proved by bisecting finitely many coordinates. On $V$ there is no such theorem, and indeed [[fs-heine-borel-holds-in-every-normed-space]] refutes the corresponding claim on the same space.
