research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-7495-items/thm-all-norms-on-rn-are-equivalent.md:117:- **The sphere is where the argument is finite-dimensional.** The step that fails outside $\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is step 3.1: closed and bounded gives compact by [[thm-heine-borel-rn]], a theorem about $\mathbb{R}^{n}$ for a natural $n$ and about nothing else. The companion page exhibits a real vector space carrying two inequivalent norms, and the same space with a closed bounded set that is not compact. **This remark is a statement about this proof and those witnesses; it makes no broader classification claim about normed spaces.**
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-7496-items/thm-all-norms-on-rn-are-equivalent.md-118-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-7497-items/thm-all-norms-on-rn-are-equivalent.md:119:- **The constants are not canonical.** Nothing in the statement fixes $c$ or $C'$, and the proof produces one admissible pair, not the best one. Sharp constants for the three named norms on $\mathbb{R}^{2}$ are computed on the companion page.
